From 54af2983f082e7e9934ec28a863a2a937713508d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 12:26:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] robo(reference): a design is the same design however the
 spaces fall
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Downloads → Reference Sheet matched designs case-insensitively but only
COLLAPSED runs of space. The register does not disagree about how many spaces
there are — it disagrees about whether there is one. Taj Vein, Tajvein,
TajVein, TAJVEIN and taj vein are one design, and only the last two matched
each other.

What that cost, in the live register on the day of the change:

  "Taj vein"  setups run to batch D-1470, last slab 17 Oct 2026
  "Tajvein"   setups stop at batch D-1398, last slab 31 Jul 2026

So an operator who typed it without the space was handed the reference sheet
for a batch nearly three months old — the wrong programs, the wrong delays —
with nothing on screen to say so. The same for "CRYSTALLO - ARLINA", whose
slabs are all under "CRYSTALLO-ARLINA": searching it with spaces round the
hyphen returned "No previous production record found".

designMatchKey now drops spacing entirely rather than collapsing it. \s in JS
covers tabs, newlines and the Unicode spaces, so a name pasted out of a sheet
folds like a typed one.

CHECKED AGAINST THE WHOLE REGISTER FIRST, because a fold that is too eager
silently merges two real designs: of 45 distinct names typed across 104
setups, folding spaces away merges exactly those two pairs and nothing else.
Only spacing is folded — a hyphen is a character the operator typed, so
"Taj-Vein" stays its own name — and the trailing-thickness rule is unchanged,
with "Statuario 5" still keeping its 5 and now keying as "Statuario5" does.

Nothing else uses designMatchKey: the change is the Reference Sheet's search
and nothing beyond it.

