From 8c67cdb293bd7aad6a2df138b83d083c62c444cc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 04:11:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] robo(reports): an hour's delays are the hour's, cut at
 the hour
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The tooltip was reading the register correctly and adding it up wrongly. On
batch D-1473, 19 Oct, the 05:00 hour listed eight D1 rows totalling 1 hour 9
minutes — of a sixty-minute hour — the last of them "26 minutes [05:41–06:07]",
which is a stretch of time the 05:00 hour mostly did not contain.

Two faults, both in how the hour was formed, neither in the data.

1 — A DELAY WAS FILED WHOLE UNDER THE HOUR IT BEGAN IN. It is now CUT at each
hour boundary and listed in every hour it ran through, keeping its code and
carrying the part of itself that fell there:

      D1 03:55 → 04:12    03:00–04:00   D1 — 5 minutes  [03:55–04:00]
                          04:00–05:00   D1 — 12 minutes [04:00–04:12]

  The register has 29 delays longer than an hour and the longest is T1
  22:00 → 06:00, 480 minutes — a power cut. All 480 went into the 22:00
  bucket, and the seven hours it actually covered — every one of them with no
  slabs, FOR THAT REASON — read "No delays". Those hours now read T1 — 60
  minutes [23:00–00:00] and so on, hour by hour, which is the owner's
  zero-slab case exactly.

2 — THE TOTAL WAS THE LINES ADDED UP. The floor logs a delay against the SLAB
  it held up, so one stoppage is written down once per slab waiting on it and
  the rows overlap: that 05:00 hour has D1 05:28–05:49 with D1 05:32–05:34 and
  D1 05:36–05:39 inside it, and D1 05:41–06:07 starting before it ends. The
  line was stopped 05:28 → 06:07, once. Total delays is now the minutes of the
  hour that were under a delay, counted once — 49 for that hour — which cannot
  exceed 60 because the pieces are clipped to the hour. Every row is still
  listed with its own times, and when they overlap the box says so rather than
  leaving a reader checking an addition that cannot work.

HOW LONG a delay ran now comes from the END TIME, the operator's own record of
when the line came back, wrapped at midnight (22:00 → 06:00 is eight hours,
not minus sixteen). Checked across all 1,783 delays on record: every one has
both times and every one has durationMinutes equal to that span, so the two
never disagree; durationMinutes stays as the fallback for a missing end.

AUDITED AGAINST THE WHOLE REGISTER, not just the batch reported. Of 628 hours
that carry a delay, 40 claimed more than sixty minutes, the worst claiming
480. After this: 0, and the worst hour claims exactly 60. 51 further hours had
a delay running through them and were reading "No delays". Re-running the real
D-1473 rows through the new code gives, hour by hour, exactly the figures an
independent SQL sweep of the same rows gives — 49 minutes for the 05:00 hour,
and all 144 slabs of the run still charted.

Slab counts, the line, the shift colours and every other report are untouched:
this changes which hour a delay is reported in and what the hour's total
means, and nothing else.

