From bf512a19661e2387cb63faf73227bd3b6cf166b3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 05:43:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] robo(reports): the shift band says how many slabs the
 shift made
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Production Rate per Hour already named each stretch of the line — SHIFT A,
SHIFT B, SHIFT C, under the hours it covers. It now also says what came off
the line in it:

        SHIFT B
        44 SLABS

The figure is the block's OWN HOURS ADDED UP — the same numbers the points
above it are drawn from, not a second query with a second opinion. That is
what makes the counting exact without a word of special-casing: a slab is
counted in the hour its Out Time falls in (hourlyProduction), every hour
belongs to exactly one shift by the plant's own windows (06:00–14:00,
14:00–22:00, 22:00–06:00 — productionShift.SHIFT_WINDOWS, unchanged), and the
blocks cover every hour of the run exactly once. So every slab is counted
once, under the shift its production time fell in, and a day's three shifts
add up to the day.

The night shift is one figure across midnight, not two — 22:00 → 06:00 is one
shift however many dates it touches — and a run only part-covering a shift is
counted over the part that was run.

TWO LINES, not "SHIFT B: 44 SLABS" on one, and the reason was measured rather
than guessed. Bold at 14px, "SHIFT B" is 70px and the whole sentence 166px,
while the chart gives every hour 52px — on one line it would need a four-hour
block to clear its neighbours, and the first and last blocks of a run are
routinely shorter. Stacked, the wider line is the count, 79px at three digits,
which two hours covers with room to spare. A one-hour end block shows the
letter and the bare number.

The name keeps the shift's colour; the count wears the dark ink the date
markers use. The band grew by one line and the chart grew with it — in
chart-tokens, where HOURLY_PLOT_H is also what the page's loading placeholder
reserves, so the two cannot drift — leaving the plot area exactly as tall as
it was.

