From 30d78f23de1dedebab7e04baaa1645ed0ee063d7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 05:56:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] attendance: an In-Time may be ten minutes old, and the
 Out-Time is stamped, not typed
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two rules, so a day cannot be written as something other than what happened.

1 — AN IN-TIME MAY BE UP TO TEN MINUTES OLD, AND NOT IN THE FUTURE. At 18:43
the earliest is 18:33, exactly as the owner put it. Ten minutes is room to
finish walking to the tablet; it is not room to write this morning in at six
o'clock this evening. The field opens ON THE CLOCK for a row that has none
yet, shows "18:33 or later" underneath, and turns red with the reason — and
Save with it — the moment anything earlier is typed.

2 — AN OUT-TIME IS NEVER TYPED. It is stamped with the clock, so it cannot be
back-dated, cannot be post-dated, and is exactly the moment the operator
pressed it. The box is a div and two buttons, not an input: there is nothing
to type in.

WHOSE CLOCK: the plant's, Asia/Kolkata, on both ends — the same reading
lib/chromia/plant-time gives the rest of the ERP. The page runs the two
functions so the operator is told at once; THE SERVER runs them again against
its own reading before anything is stored, because a page can be edited and a
request can be made without one. The browser never decides what is stored.

Both comparisons go the SHORT way round the clock, so 23:58 is two minutes
before 00:00 rather than twenty-three hours and fifty-eight minutes after it:
the night shift marks in across midnight every night and must not meet a wall
when it does. A tablet running up to two minutes fast is believed, or ordinary
drift would refuse every honest entry for a reason nobody on the floor could
see; an Out-Time, which is the one the record really turns on, is not typed at
all and so is never in the future by a second.

THE RULE IS ABOUT ENTERING A TIME, NOT CARRYING ONE. An In-Time that did not
change is left alone however old it is — otherwise opening this morning's row
in the evening to tick the other line would send its 06:00 back, be refused,
and the row could never be corrected again. The page and the server spare it
by the same test, or the page would refuse to send what the server would have
accepted.

One register, two ERPs: this is the one handler both doors reach, so the rules
are the same on the Robo tablet and the Chromia one by construction.

