From e2cde958c72942384bf0c571a3f8a33573996008 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 06:01:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] attendance: the roster is the owner's twelve, in the
 owner's order
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The register sorted alphabetically. The floor's list is not alphabetical — it
is the order the line is called in, and the owner gave it as a numbered list:

    1 Jagadeesh      5 Praveen S.    9 Sebul
    2 Rajesh         6 Venkatraj    10 Shiva
    3 Saravanan .L   7 U Kumar      11 Mani Bharathi
    4 Kiran          8 Aaquib       12 Yogender Reddy

Alphabetical would open with Aaquib, which is a different document from the one
the floor keeps. So the position is now data: "sortOrder" on the operator, and
the only thing any reader orders by.

  • listOperators returns [sortOrder asc, id asc] — the screen, the + Add
    Operator panel and the Downloads operator picker all read that one list.
  • the sheet orders [date, operator.sortOrder, operatorId], so a day reads
    down the page exactly as the register reads on screen.
  • compareOperatorNames is deleted rather than left unused: exported, it is
    one import away from sorting the register again.
  • a new operator is appended (max + 1), read INSIDE the add's own
    transaction so two tablets adding at once cannot take the same number;
    and a rename may write name and isActive only, so editing a name cannot
    reorder the list.

Add, Rename and Remove are untouched and still work on every operator, trail
and all, and because both ERPs were already one handler over one table this is
one roster: a name added, renamed or removed on the Robo side is the same row
the Chromia side reads.

scripts/0137 adds the column; the owner's numbering is DATA, not schema, so
prisma/seed-attendance.ts holds the twelve and numbers them 1..12 (npm run
db:seed:attendance, idempotent). The column and the twelve are already on Neon
from 20 Oct — the script is IF NOT EXISTS, so it is the record of what was
done and what a fresh database runs.

Spelling is the owner's, down to the space in "Saravanan .L" and the stop in
"Praveen S." — a tidy-up would quietly create a thirteenth operator next time
the seed ran.

tests/roboShiftAttendance0123 held the schema block to 0123 column for column;
a column added later had no way to exist. It now reads the later scripts too,
named one at a time, so the model is still exactly what the scripts built and
a new attendance column is a line in that test as well as a script.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01SPqDFZ7o6eq1utS7hzoQsF
